Good — this is the right architectural question. Let’s slow it down and answer it cleanly.

You’re asking:

Do I need to install external crawler / deep research packages and connect via API?
Or can I build everything “in-house” inside Cloudflare Workers?

The answer depends on what level of scraping you want.

🧠 1️⃣ What Cloudflare Workers Can Do Natively

Inside Workers you can:

Fetch HTML pages (fetch())

Parse HTML (with a lightweight parser like linkedom or cheerio-compatible edge version)

Call OpenAI / AI APIs

Stream responses

Store results in D1

Store blobs in R2

That means:

✅ Basic web scraping → Fully in-house
✅ AI synthesis → Fully in-house
✅ Deep research workflow orchestration → Fully in-house
✅ Streaming AI → Fully in-house

No external microservices required.

🚫 2️⃣ What Workers Cannot Do

Workers cannot run a headless browser like:

Puppeteer

Playwright

Chromium

Selenium

That means:

❌ You cannot render heavy JS apps (like React-heavy sites) inside Workers
❌ You cannot bypass advanced bot protections
❌ You cannot simulate full browser sessions

This is a hard runtime limitation.

🔥 So What Are Your Real Options?
OPTION A — 100% In-House (Edge-Only)

Architecture:

Cloudflare Worker
 ├── /scrape → fetch HTML + parse
 ├── /research → orchestrate multi-source scrape
 ├── /ai → streaming synthesis
 ├── D1 → metadata
 └── R2 → attachments


Pros:

Clean

Cheap

Scales infinitely

Minimal latency

Cons:

Won’t scrape JS-heavy sites

Won’t bypass anti-bot protections

For 70–80% of research use cases? This works.

OPTION B — Hybrid Model (Recommended for You)

Keep Workers as the brain.

Add a headless scraping microservice somewhere else:

Small VPS

Your Synology NAS

Cloudflare Browser Rendering (if using Enterprise)

Or a separate Playwright worker

Architecture:

Worker (Orchestrator)
   ↓
Headless Scraper Service (Playwright)
   ↓
Return rendered HTML
   ↓
Worker parses + AI synthesizes


Pros:

Full scraping power

JS-rendered pages work

Still Cloudflare-first

Cons:

Slightly more complex

Requires maintaining one extra service

🧠 3️⃣ For Deep Research Specifically

Deep Research is not one thing.

It’s:

Topic expansion

Search

URL discovery

Page fetching

Parsing

Ranking

AI synthesis

Storage

All of that orchestration logic absolutely can live inside one Worker.

The only piece that might need external support is JS-rendered scraping.

🖥 4️⃣ For Your Clipboard Vision (Important)

Since you want:

Windows AutoHotkey

Android automation

PWA

Cloudflare backend

Always-online

You do NOT want scraping logic inside your desktop app.

You want:

Devices → Cloudflare Worker API → D1/R2 → AI


All intelligence centralized at the edge.

💰 5️⃣ Do You Need to Install Packages?

If staying Cloudflare-native:

You don’t “install” packages like Node does.

You add edge-compatible libraries via:

wrangler.toml

ES modules

Bundled lightweight parsers

No system installs.
No servers.
No docker.
No background daemon.

Everything deploys with wrangler deploy.

🎯 My Direct Recommendation For You

Given your long-term infrastructure goals:

Build 90% inside Workers.

Only add a separate headless scraping microservice if:

You hit JS-heavy research targets regularly.

You need anti-bot bypass.

You need authenticated scraping.

Otherwise?

Keep it edge-native.

Clean.
Fast.
Sovereign.

